# Stone Game IX

## Problem Statement

Alice and Bob take turns removing stones from a pile.

Each stone has an integer value. On each turn, a player removes one stone and adds its value to the current sum.

If the sum becomes divisible by 3, the player who made that move loses.

The game continues until no stones remain.

Given an integer array `stones`, return `true` if Alice can win assuming both players play optimally, otherwise return `false`.

## Approach

The actual values of the stones are not important. Only their remainder when divided by `3` matters.

We divide all stones into three groups:

- `cnt[0]` → stones where `stone % 3 == 0`
- `cnt[1]` → stones where `stone % 3 == 1`
- `cnt[2]` → stones where `stone % 3 == 2`

### Key Observations

If the number of remainder-0 stones is even:

- Alice can win only if there is at least one remainder-1 stone and one remainder-2 stone.

If the number of remainder-0 stones is odd:

- Alice wins when the difference between the number of remainder-1 and remainder-2 stones is greater than `2`.

Therefore:

```cpp
if (cnt[0] % 2 == 0) {
    return cnt[1] > 0 && cnt[2] > 0;
}

return abs(cnt[1] - cnt[2]) > 2;
